ove and hope for the best.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state shows that White has a powerful threat on the diagonal. Specifically, White has four connected stones at F10, G11, H12, and I13. This formation is blocked at the top-left by Black\'s stone at E9, but it is completely open at the bottom-right at intersection J14. This is a "Sleep Four" or "Four-in-a-row" threat. If Black does not play at J14 immediately, White will play there next turn to complete a 5-in-a-row and win the game.\n\nI have analyzed Black\'s potential offensive lines to see if there is a faster win (a VCF or immediate 4/5):\n- The black diagonal J6-I7-H8-G9 is blocked at both ends (K5 and F10).\n- The black diagonal F11-G10-H9-I8 is blocked at both ends (E12 and J7).\n- The black vertical line H8-H9-H10-H11 is blocked at both ends (H7 and H12).\n- The black diagonal L7-K8-J9 is blocked at I10.\n\nSince Black has no immediate winning moves to counter-attack or ignore the threat, Black is forced to play defensively.\n\nReason: White has a diagonal 4-in-a-row (F10-I13) that will become a winning 5-in-a-row at J14 if not blocked. Black has no faster winning threats, so blocking at J14 is the necessary move to avoid losing immediately.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 14}'}
