iagonally, by J6-K7-L8, with an open path for future extension.\n\nThis double threat forces white to make a defensive choice on their next move. White can only block one line, leaving the other one open for me. I realized any line can be countered, so I needed to have two lines to counter each White\'s potential response.\n\nI also considered other moves. I checked F13, that would create a Sleeping 3. This threat isn\'t strong because White can easily block it, and F13 can\'t create a real Open 3. I also checked L6-L7-L8, but that could also easily be blocked. I checked other lines like K7 but K7 creates a weak position.\n\nThe move L8 is the most strategic move in the current situation, giving me the most opportunity for the future.\n\nTherefore, my move will be at column L, row 8.\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state shows that Black has several clusters of stones, but most of them are heavily blocked by White\'s stones. Specifically, the vertical lines in columns G, H, and K are capped at both ends (e.g., K10-K13 is blocked by K9 and K14). White does not appear to have any immediate lethal threats (4-in-a-row or Open 3). Therefore, Black must find a constructive move to create new threats.\n\nThe move at F13 was considered to extend the F11-F12 line, but since F10 is occupied by White, this would only create a "sleeping 3" which can be easily blocked at F14, rendering it useless.\n\nThe most promising area is around the stones J6, L7, and K8.\nThe move at **L8** is excellent for the following reasons:\n1.  **Vertical Connection:** It connects with L7 to form a vertical "Live 2" (L7-L8) with open ends at L6 and L9.\n2.  **Diagonal Connection:** It connects with J6 to form a "Split Live 2" (J6 - empty - L8). Filling the gap at K7 would create a strong Live 3.\n3.  **Horizontal Connection:** It forms a connection with K8.\n\nBy creating two distinct potential lines (vertical and diagonal) that share the L8 stone but require different defensive moves